  "canonical": "/dataset/jobs-and-job-density-borough-2jk0d",
  "nextReviewDate": null,
  "createdAt": "2014-09-16T15:35:13.000Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-05-06T08:18:37.131Z",
  "archivedAt": null,
  "licence": {
   "url": "https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/2/",
   "title": "Open Government Licence v2"
  },
  "contact": {
   "email": "datastore@london.gov.uk",
   "title": "Greater London Authority"
  },
  "description": "<dp-prose>\n  <p>Data shows the number of jobs and job density by borough.</p>\n  <p>The number of jobs in an area is composed of jobs done by residents (of any age) and jobs done by workers (of any age) who commute into the area.</p>\n  <p>Total jobs is a workplace based measure of jobs and comprises:</p>\n  <p>- employees (from the Annual Business Inquiry),</p>\n  <p>- self-employment jobs (from the Annual Population Survey), People who are self-employed in a second job are included in the self-employed totals.</p>\n  <p>- government-supported trainees (from DfES and DWP) and</p>\n  <p>- HM Forces (from MoD).</p>\n  <p>Job density is the number of jobs per resident of working age (male and female: 16-64). For example, a job density of 1.0 would mean that there is one job for every resident of working age in the population.</p>\n  <p>More information on jobs available in <a href=\"/dataset/workplace-employment-sex-and-status-borough\">Workplace Employment by Sex and Status, Borough</a> and modelled estimates and projections of jobs are available in the <a href=\"/dataset/long-term-labour-market-projections/\">GLA Employment Projections.</a> These are considered to be the most accurate jobs estimates at borough level.</p>\n  <p>Download this data from <a href=\"https://www.nomisweb.co.uk/\">NOMIS</a></p>\n</dp-prose>",
